{"classes":["","diverted","regulated","reservoir"],"exclude":["diverted","reservoir"],"notes":{"diverted":"Hydro-diverted: most of the natural flow is taken out upstream, so the natural flood statistic overstates the flow.","regulated":"Flow controlled by a hydro scheme (storage dam, control gates or tailrace): natural flood statistics do not describe it.","reservoir":"Hydro lake or dam structure, not a river reach."},"schemes":[{"id":"piriaka","name":"Piriaka","operator":"King Country Energy","note":"Small run-of-river station (1924; 1-1.5 MW at 8.2 m head) on the Whanganui near Manunui: a weir and a canal bypass part of the river. Approx: the weir position and the share taken (take = 0.6) are estimates. At low flow the station took 10 m3/s with at least 1.5 m3/s left over the weir (11.5 m3/s at Piriaka: NIWA Fisheries Environmental Report 22, via a web-search excerpt), about 0.87 of the river; the station's capacity and the river's median flow at Piriaka were not found (rf-6u2; EA Report 4 and its attributes table do not cover Piriaka), so the share at normal flows stays an estimate.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_power_stations_in_New_Zealand","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piriaka_Power_Station","https://www.kce.co.nz/history-of-piriaka-power-station/","https://webstatic.niwa.co.nz/library/fer22.pdf"]},{"id":"tongariro","name":"Tongariro Power Scheme","operator":"Genesis Energy","note":"Western Diversion: the Whakapapa intake and a 16.5 km tunnel to Lake Te Whaiau, with intake dams on the Okupata, Taurewa, Tawhitikuri and Mangatepopo streams on the way; the Whanganui headwaters go by a short tunnel to the Te Whaiau Stream; Lake Otamangakau and the Wairehu Canal take it all to Lake Rotoaira. Eastern Diversion: Lake Moawhango (Moawhango River, 22 Whangaehu tributaries) to the Tongariro by a 19 km tunnel; Rangipo Dam diverts the Tongariro to Rangipo Power Station, whose 3 km tailrace tunnel returns it just above the Poutu Intake, which sends the river to Lake Rotoaira and Tokaanu (Lake Taupo). Not modelled: the Whangaehu-side aqueduct intakes, the Waihohonu intake (EA Report 4, 2.2: the Tongariro section begins at it), and the Taurewa and Tawhitikuri intakes (about 2 m3/s each). EA Report 4's Figure 2-3 (Genesis schematic, p. 4) shows the Taurewa and Tawhitikuri intakes on the Western Diversion between the Okupata and Mangatepopo intakes (from the west: Whakapapa, Okupata, Taurewa, Tawhitikuri, Mangatepopo), not to scale; no source found gives where they are (the Tawhitikuri Stream is named in REC2, up to 8.4 km2; no stream is named Taurewa in REC2 or the LINZ Topo50 river names), rf-6u2.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tongariro_Power_Scheme","https://teara.govt.nz/en/map/11834/tongariro-power-development","https://genesisenergy.co.nz/about/generation/rivers-lakes-and-rainfall","https://www.horizons.govt.nz/HRC/media/Media/One%20Plan/Jarrod-Bowler-for-Genesis-Power-Ltd.pdf?ext=.pdf","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/1_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes/HMD%20Report%204%20-%20Hydro-power%20Schemes%20Background%20and%20Descriptions.pdf"]}],"r":[[7768,1,1,95],[7769,1,1,95],[7770,1,1,95],[7771,1,1,95],[7812,1,1,94],[7813,1,1,94],[7814,1,1,93],[7828,1,1,92],[7829,1,1,92],[7830,1,1,92],[7915,1,1,92],[7919,1,1,92],[7920,1,1,92],[7921,1,1,92],[8021,1,1,90],[8033,1,1,90],[8034,1,1,90],[8035,1,1,90],[8160,1,1,89],[8161,1,1,89],[8175,1,1,88],[8176,1,1,87],[18172,1,0,74],[18182,1,0,74],[18304,1,0,74],[18305,1,0,74],[18306,1,0,74],[18307,1,0,74],[18308,1,0,74],[18309,1,0,74],[18467,1,0,74],[18468,1,0,74],[18469,1,0,74],[19088,1,1,50],[19089,1,1,50],[19090,1,1,50],[19091,1,1,50],[19104,1,1,50],[19175,1,1,51],[19176,1,1,51],[19177,1,1,51],[19178,1,1,51],[19195,1,1,51],[19226,1,1,52],[19227,1,1,52],[19230,3,1,100],[19231,3,1,100],[19232,3,1,100],[19233,3,1,100],[19235,3,1,100],[19239,1,1,52],[19240,1,1,52],[19241,1,1,52],[19264,3,1,100],[19265,1,1,52],[19266,1,1,52],